# Day 2 Reflection

## What Happened

Today I worked on the Northstar project and started implementing the frontend based on the requirements we had discussed. As I progressed, I realized that I had not followed the correct user journey from the previous meeting.

I had focused on placing the different features and forms on the page, but I had not clearly understood how the user was supposed to move from one part of the system to another. This made me realize that I needed to understand the workflow before continuing with the implementation.

Because of this, I asked the team whether we could have a simple sketch or use **Excalidraw** to draw the workflow. I felt that having a visual representation of the user journey would make it easier for me to understand the relationship between the screens and implement the frontend correctly.

## What Went Well

I was able to identify the problem before proceeding too far with the implementation. I also communicated the issue with the team instead of making assumptions about how the system should flow.

The experience helped me understand that the user journey should guide the frontend structure rather than simply putting all the required features onto one page.

## What Was Harder Than Expected

The challenging part was translating the requirements from the meeting into an actual frontend flow. I initially understood the individual features but missed how they were supposed to connect.

I also realized that having a visual workflow would have helped me understand the requirements much earlier.

## Self-Assessment

I would rate my performance at **7/10**. I made progress on the project, but I could have avoided some of the rework by making sure I fully understood the user journey before starting the implementation.

My main lesson today was that when working on a team project, I should first understand the workflow and system structure before writing the code. A simple sketch or workflow diagram can make the requirements much clearer and help prevent unnecessary rework.
